    "story": "He does not think of it as being honored. He thinks of it as reporting. The names read out at these gatherings belonged to men who stood where he stood and did not get to be old, and somebody from the unit should be present when the flags come out. So every year: the cap, the blazer, the whole bright arithmetic of ribbons that says where he was so he never has to. The cane is recent. The duty is not. When the speeches start he will stand for them, whatever it costs. That part has never been in question.",
    "notes": "The blazer does the archive's work: every ribbon and patch is a citation the sitter earned and declines to narrate, arranged across his chest like a table of contents to a war. Compositionally the portrait is frontal and seated, almost formal, the cane held vertically between the hands with a stillness that recalls parade rest. It stands out in the collection for its gravity - a portrait that is also a primary source, of a generation whose commemorations are becoming memorials. Families and veterans' halls recognize it instantly for what it is: presence, reported for duty.",
