    "story": "Everybody backstage has a version of me they prefer, and every one of them is wrong in a useful way. The blue light is honest, at least. It doesn't warm anybody up. Out there they want the menace or the mystery, depending on the night, and I can do either before my tea cools. What I can't do - what the contract of this face doesn't cover - is tell them which one was mine. Some nights I don't know either. Break's over.",
    "notes": "The crop is the argument: face as landscape, no costume context left to hide behind, the goggles riding the brim like a second, unreadable pair of eyes. Blue light does the color grading a painter would call a limited palette - skin gone porcelain, lips nearly black - and the intensity holds because nothing in the frame offers relief. It is the companion frame to the same character's gasp portrait: the playfulness has clocked out, and what replaced it is considerably harder to look away from.",
